"""
cap_assign_gmsh_to_simvascular.py
=================================

End-to-end utility for:

1) transferring SimVascular ModelFaceID labels from an existing reference
   model onto a new CLOSED pulmonary-artery surface,
2) diagnosing degenerate/sliver/duplicate/self-intersecting surface patches,
3) tetrahedralizing that surface with Gmsh, and
4) exporting a SimVascular-compatible mesh-complete directory.

Expected inputs
---------------
BASE_VTP
    Original/reference surface containing ModelFaceID cell data.
OLD_FACENAMES
    SimVascular .facenames file paired with BASE_VTP.
RESULT_VTP
    New CLOSED/watertight surface that should be volume meshed.  Despite the
    historical variable name, this can be any PyVista-readable polygonal
    surface such as .vtp or a Meshmixer-exported .stl.  It does not need
    ModelFaceID data; the script reassigns it from the reference caps.

Primary output
--------------
MESH_COMPLETE_DIR/
    mesh-complete.mesh.vtu
    mesh-complete.exterior.vtp
    walls_combined.vtp
    mesh-surfaces/
        <one .vtp file for every face name>

The mesh-complete files include the SimVascular arrays:
    volume mesh:
        PointData GlobalNodeID
        CellData  GlobalElementID
        CellData  ModelRegionID
    boundary meshes:
        PointData GlobalNodeID
        CellData  GlobalElementID  (owning tetrahedron ID)
        CellData  ModelFaceID

Dependencies
------------
    pip install numpy pyvista vtk gmsh scipy
    (scipy is optional for diagnostics clustering, but recommended)

Notes
-----
* RESULT_VTP MUST be closed.  If you are using the pruning workflow developed
  earlier, use a separately capped/closed copy for meshing rather than the
  final open QC surface.
* This script intentionally generates FIRST-ORDER tetrahedra.
* Gmsh is used only to generate the tetrahedral volume mesh.  Face identity is
  transferred back from the labelled input surface after meshing, which avoids
  relying on Gmsh's STL surface-patch numbering to match SimVascular faces.
* The default Gmsh strategy PRESERVES the existing watertight STL triangles and
  asks Gmsh only to fill the enclosed volume with tetrahedra.  This avoids the
  classifySurfaces()/createGeometry() reparametrization step that can fail on
  highly distorted STL triangles.  The older reparametrization workflow remains
  available as an optional fallback.
"""

